#  Makefile for compiling NetHack3.0 with Microsoft C 5.1 -- Paul Gyugyi
#
#  Well, I got a lot of requests to post my makefile.  Thanks for all
#  the help in finding a patch program for the IBM PC.
#
#  This is the first of three postings.  Part one is the instructions,
#  part two is the makefile, part three the make.ini file.
#
#  The "#" to the left are because this used to be a header for my makefile.
#
#  This is a one step makefile.  You type "make all" and it does
#  EVERYTHING for you.  I represents several days of getting Nethack
#  to compile.  It will compile the termcap library for you.  It will
#  compile lev_comp.exe for you.  It will make the special levels, and
#  copy everything to your game directory.  For this convenience, you
#  pay a price:  you must have your disks and software set up just like mine.
#  This explanation is aimed at the novice, like me one week ago, and
#  the more experienced of you can adjust everything at your will.
#
#  Here is the software you will need:
#
#  1) You will need the Microsoft C 5.1 (I think 5.0 will work as well)
#     compiler.  Do not try to use 4.x, as the msc syntax is different.
#     You must SETUP the C compiler to create the combined large model 
#     library (llibce.lib?).  If you don't have this library on your disk,
#     run SETUP /L on the setup disk from C 5.1.
#
#  2) You will need NDMAKE.  The Microsoft make.exe is just too limited
#     to handle this task.  Rename the Microsoft make.exe to msmake.exe
#     and copy NDMAKE to make.exe in your C executable directory.  NDMAKE
#     uses a make.ini file to configure itself.  I have included my make.ini
#     at the end of this article, but be warned that the makefile overrides
#     most of the defaults, so changing your make.ini may not have any effect.
#
#  3) You have to have the nethack 3.0 source arranged according to the
#     description below.  This makefile will work with both the unpatched
#     code and with the patch1 applied.  If you can, apply the patches.
#
#  4) You will need Fred Fish's termcap.arc file included in the nethack
#     level 1 patches.  If you have a previous version of termcap, it will
#     work fine probably.  Just place ltermlib.lib (large model) in the
#     SRC subdirectory.
#
#  5) OK.  Here's how to set up your source.  First, get the source
#     from an archive site, like ftp to uunet.uu.net and get the files
#     part??.Z from comp.sources.games/volume7/nethack3.  On a UNIX
#     machine, uncompress the *.Z files, and then sh them.  (Instructions
#     are provided with the files).  Then get the patches from uunet
#     and apply them.  You should have a root directory, with the following
#     subdirectories: AMIGA, AUXIL, INCLUDE, OTHERS, and SRC.  Now copy
#     these files down to a pc.  First, don't bother with the AMIGA files,
#     you will not need them.  Put all your top level files into a subdir
#     on your hard disk.  Make 3 subdirectories: SRC, INCLUDE, AUXIL.
#     copy the AUXIL files to the AUXIL dir.  Copy the SRC files to the
#     SRC dir.  Also copy the INCLUDE files to the SRC dir.  Copy the INCLUDE
#     files to the INCLUDE dir.  Now, uudecode the termcap library provided
#     in the patches and unarc the *.c files.  You will not need the other
#     files, and don't let them overwrite anything.  Place these *.c files
#     in the SRC subdirectory.  Now copy the makefile. and make.ini files
#     I'm posting overtop the existing files in the SRC directory.
#
#  6) Now for some UNOFFICIAL bug fixes you will need to do.  If you have
#     the unpatched files, there is a bug in the zap.c file you will
#     encounter when compiling.  A ")" is missing.  don't worry about
#     it now.  Later when you do a "make all" the compiler will churn away,
#     and eventually bomb at this error.  Make a note of the line number,
#     fix up the file, and type "make all" and it will pick up where it
#     left off.  If you are using the patch1'd files, you may hit a bug
#     in bones.c if you don't define ZEROCOMPRESS.  Line 243 should be
#     surrounded with "#ifdef ZEROCOMPRESS  and #endif".  The termcap source
#     file tgoto.c should have a "*out = '\0';" inserted before the
#     return call in the first procedure.  The termcap file should have
#     the ansi entries changed from "cm=\E[%i%d%dH:" to "cm=\E[%i%2%2H:"
#     That covers all the bug I ran into when compiling.
#
#  7) Now the fun part, choosing what options you want to have.
#     First modify the pcconf.h file in the INCLUDE dir.  Comment
#     out DGK.  You need this to be defined, but I do it on the compiler
#     command line so it works with the termcap library compilation.
#     Comment out RANDOM, or you'll have to modify my makefile.
#
#     Now attack the config.h file.  Comment out
#     the #define UNIX line.  Don't bother uncommenting the #define MSDOS
#     as the C compiler defines this automatically.  Comment out LOGFILE
#     and NEWS entries, and comment out the HACKDIR line.  Comment out
#     COMPRESS option, and I'd recomment commenting out ZEROCOMPRESS also.
#     Now for the options.  I've found you must define MEDUSA with the
#     unpatched code.  I also strongly recommend EXPLORE_MODE, which is
#     very useful in making sure your executable works reliably.  Try it with
#     just these options first.  I know it may seem like a waste of two hours,
#     but this will insure you have a working executable to start with.
#     I've compiled with SPELLS,HARD,FOUNTAINS,MEDUSA,ARMY,THEOLOGY,SOUNDS,
#     EXPLORE_MODE,ALTARS,SEDUCE,STRONGHOLD and this produces an executable
#     which is 533K long and will run reliable in 560K of free memory.
#
# 7.5)If you do not have a 286 machine, go in and edit my makefile on
#     line 12 or so and remove the /G2 option.
#
#  8) Now the long part.  Change dir to the SRC subdir, and type "make all".
#     This will compile everything you need, and copy all neccessary file
#     to \games\nethack and also create a BONES subdir to hold the levels
#     and bones files.
#     You can expect to see warnings during this process.  I have turned
#     off compiler warning so your screen doesn't get filled with warnings
#     about passing pointers to constants into variables.  If you get an
#     error breaking the compile, refer to part 6 above.  When running
#     lev_comp to make special levels (If that's defined) you will see
#     warnings--they're ok.  The whole process takes about 2 hours on my
#     386 which has a REALLY SLOW hard disk.

#  9) Any questions, mail me at gyugyi@portia.stanford.edu


